#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Walk the source library and emit what a reader needs to read it.

`tools/source-library` validates some four hundred and eighty works, six hundred
editions and well over a thousand passages, and until this tool existed none of
it could be read. A passage record holds a checked transcription, its locus, its
evidence states and the exact artifact that controls it; the only way to see one
was to open a TOML file and know which one to open. Every count below is stated
as a proportion rather than a figure, because the corpus grows weekly and a
number written into prose here would be wrong within the month; `check` prints
the current ones.

    check      nothing withheld is emitted, and the page's derivation replayed
    list       the corpus, filtered by author, language, category or rights
    show       one work or edition in full, with every passage it carries
    structure  write the spine, the per-edition files and the text payloads

THE RIGHTS RULE IS THE WHOLE OF THIS TOOL, so it is stated once, here, and
every verb reads it from `reading_of` below rather than deciding for itself.

    A passage may be read here only when the artifact that controls it carries
    an affirmative recorded distribution basis AND is stored `tracked`.

Both halves are read from the record and neither is inferred from the bytes.
`guidance/sources.md` is explicit that "`tracked` requires an affirmative
recorded distribution basis; online availability and successful download are
insufficient" — so the presence of a file on disk decides nothing, and an
artifact whose rights are `restricted` or `unresolved` is refused even when its
bytes are sitting in the tree. That refusal reaches the large majority of the
corpus — roughly nine passages in ten — which is the honest answer for a body of
material this much of which is under copyright or has never had its rights
settled. A reader is owed that proportion plainly rather than a page that looks
complete.

A REFUSAL IS PUBLISHED, NEVER HIDDEN. Every passage the rule withholds is still
listed, still named by its locus, still carries its editorial context and its
evidence states, and states in place of the text which of the reasons below
applies and what the record says about it. A blank where a text would be is
indistinguishable from a text that is empty; a quiet omission is worse, because
the reader cannot tell that anything is missing at all.

WHAT COUNTS AS TEXT is narrower than "bytes we may distribute", and the second
rule is why. Only two things are set as prose:

  * a passage's own `text` — a transcription the record carries, which
    `source-library` has already validated segment by segment against the
    hash-pinned bytes it claims to reproduce; and
  * the physical lines of a `text/plain` artifact, where the passage bounds them
    with `physical_line_ranges`.

Several dozen passages are bounded into `text/tab-separated-values` artifacts
and are NOT read here, though the rights would allow it. A TSV row is a structured
record — key, slot, incipit, two page numbers, then the words — and setting one
as prose would print its keys and its pagination as though they were part of the
prayer. The propers browser reads those artifacts through the schema they
actually have, which is where that content belongs; this tool says so instead of
approximating it.

The library is read through `tools/source-library`, which is the one reader of
these records and the thing that validates them. A second walk of the same TOML
would be a second answer waiting to differ from the first — this repository has
already paid for that, with two copies of the same propers that diverged in five
ways before anything compared them. This file decides only what a reader sees.

Nothing is scraped in the browser. The page fetches what `structure` writes.
"""
